[Music]  North Korean leader Kim Jong-un has been  trying to build a new image for himself  he's gotten a new wardrobe  and lost some weight  he's also been showing emotion  [Applause]  while still being the commander-in-chief  who oversees epic missile launches he's  going for versatility North Korea's  propaganda strategy under Kim Jong-un  are modernization and transparency  the shift comes as the regime is facing  a lot of challenges at home North Korean  analysts say adapting the regime's  propaganda is one way to keep the  message under control  so We examined recent footage that shows  Kim's efforts to modernize what his  predecessors did as he redefines what it  means to be the leader of one of the  world's most isolated regimes  Kim Jong-un is the third leader of North  Korea after his father and grandfather  he took power in late 2011 when he was  believed to be just 27 years old Kim  Jong-un started off building his Public  Image modeled on his grandfather Rachel  Lee is a former North Korea analyst for  the U.S government who now works for a  website that provides analysis about the  regime  until recently Kim Jong-un had been  borrowing from his grandfather's  propaganda Playbook  the way he likes to give public speeches  when you see Kim Jong-un going out  visiting places or giving inspections  you see him mingling with people so when  people see him they're reminded of Kim  also but in recent years he's broken out  of that shell now and he's trying to  build his own brand and this rebranding  comes amid the country's mounting  economic problems as a result of ongoing  sanctions and the pandemic lockdown plus  there's been major flooding and crop  failures that have led to food shortages  estate runs South Korean Think Tank  estimates that the north needs more than  5 million tons of food annually the last  year it could have faced food shortages  of more than one million tons that food  deficit means an average North Korean is  getting around 450 calories fewer than  what's recommended by the United Nations  Lee says citizens won't believe the  regime if all these issues are covered  up so North Korea has really made an  effort to be more transparent to be  persuasive to the people and also to  make the content more interesting  to do that Kim's been attempting to  break the mold by trying new things  like in this highly edited video that  showcases the launch of what Pyongyang  described as a Next Generation  intercontinental ballistic missile  in walking out in front of a missile you  know that's something that you would see  maybe in a mission an impossible movie  this is a big departure from previous  weapons tests where Kim often watched  from inside a control room  and the event was announced by a news  anchor describing what happened  Lee says this kind of elaborate  production can drum up support for the  military even amid questions about the  regime's weapons capability  about a week after the launch South  Korean officials questioned whether the  ICBM was really new saying it appeared  to be just a dressed up version of a  predecessor  and this stylized video could also have  been produced to mollify the North  Korean public about a missile test in  mid-march that South Korea's military  said had failed they went out all the  way to make the video more special to  kind of bending over backwards right  overcompensating for what happened but  if they continue to do this then maybe  this is the beginning of a new trend  Pyongyang hasn't commented on the test  or the ICBM launch skepticism  what's also changing is the clothing and  Kim's various outfits are a stark  contrast to his father and grandfather  they normally stuck to a uniform whether  it was a suit and necktie or Mouse suits  which were popularized by communist  China's first leader  Kim has also allowed t-shirts that North  Korea experts say appeared to have his  face emblazoned on it like at this  concert last fall it's something that  you see all the Time in the West and  maybe it was somebody's idea to become  more modernized Lee says this type of  casual iconography would never have  happened during the regime under Kim's  father and grandfather because  propaganda has always portrayed the  leaders as sacred and Powerful Godlike  figures so this modernizing of content  marks a turning point and could come  from a desire for Kim to reach a younger  generation they're more knowledgeable  about the outside world a huge portion  of the population that has been exposed  to dramas and movies hence the need to  modernize content and it has to evolve  so that people keep tuning into National  Television another part of kin's  propaganda change more transparency  especially about the food shortage this  was also something that Kim's father had  to manage during his tenure at the time  there was no real acknowledgment of its  severity even though as many as one  million people in North Korea had died  North Korean State media used terms like  food shortages and food problem you  would see more watered-down account of  what was going on eventually the U.S and  United Nations stepped in to provide  food Aid and helped to bring the famine  to an end by the late 1990s  but fast forward some 20 years and Kim  Jong-un is going about it differently by  openly talking about the food situation  that the country faces today  this documentary called the great year  of victory in 2021 aired on state media  in February the narrator says the phrase  food crisis something that North Korea  analysts say has never been uttered  before in the country's propaganda  while Kim has said the country is  investing in agriculture and making  progress on the food shortage he's also  been physically showing citizens that he  understands their struggles  last June he appeared at a party meeting  looking considerably thinner and news of  his weight loss was widely reported on  state media  [Applause]  and also you see Kim Jong-un giving a  speech and he was in tears and he was  choking up as he talked about the  hardship that people had to go through  North Korea analysts say it's hard to  tell if this new propaganda style is  working and whether younger people are  relating to it more than earlier forms  ultimately propaganda is not about  getting anyone's approval the shaping  and controlling people's perception of  the leadership especially during  challenging times as Kim enters the  second decade of his rule  foreign

